<script>
	import Callout from '$lib/components/Callout.svelte';
</script>

ApiTool has built-in request signers — no plugin binary required. The `signing:`
block applies between variable templating and dispatch, so you sign requests to AWS
services with **Signature V4** using credentials that come from a vault, not from the
YAML. Because those credentials are vault-resolved, they're redacted in every output.

## Credentials from a vault

```yaml
# apitest.yaml
secrets:
  provider: aws-secrets-manager
  region: us-east-1
  keys:
    aws_key: "prod/aws#access_key_id"        # #field extracts one key from the secret
    aws_secret: "prod/aws#secret_access_key"
  cache_ttl: 300
  refresh_on_failure: true
```

ApiTool shells out to the provider's authenticated CLI (`aws` here) — it doesn't
embed SDKs. The `#field` suffix pulls a single field out of a structured secret.

## Sign the request

```yaml
# collections/s3.yaml
name: S3 access
requests:
  - name: List bucket
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "https://s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/my-bucket?list-type=2"
    signing:
      type: aws-sigv4
      params:
        region: us-east-1
        service: s3
        access_key: "{{aws_key}}"
        secret_key: "{{aws_secret}}"   # vault-resolved → auto-redacted
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Content-Type:
          matches: "^application/xml"
```

STS-issued temporary credentials work too — add `session_token: "{{aws_session}}"`.

## Run it

```bash
apitest run collections/s3.yaml
```

```
Collection: S3 access
  ✓ List bucket  200  208ms

────────────────────────────────
  1 request(s): 1 passed, 0 failed (208ms)
```

<Callout type="output">
	Representative output — signing against your own AWS account. For local runs without
	hitting a real vault, set <code>APITEST_VAULT_STUB=1</code> to use an in-memory stub.
</Callout>

<Callout type="warn">
	Always reference credentials as <code>{'{{vars}}'}</code>, never as literal strings.
	A literal <code>secret_key</code> has no source variable to trace back to, so
	ApiTool can't add it to the redaction set — and it could leak into output. The
	vault-backed approach above is redacted by construction.
</Callout>
